  "body": "## Result\n\nFor every **even** rotation period `n`, the formal `F2[D_{2n}]` register model with paid reflections only `x,y`, arbitrary binary rank-one idempotents, free scalar mixing, and any finite restored dirty workspace has the sharp separate bills for heterogeneous diagonal targets `r^k_i x^e_i`:\n\n- x calls: `sum d_n(k_i+e_i)`\n- y calls: `sum d_n(k_i)`\n\nHere `r=xy`, `e_i in {0,1}`, and `d_n` is cyclic distance to zero. Coordinate words simultaneously attain both bounds, hence all nonnegative per-letter tariffs are exact.\n\nThe uniform lower-bound proof uses two n-address permutation representations with offsets `c=n/2` and `c=n/2-1`, plus subadditive balanced-cut ranks. The offsets matter for `n=2 mod 4`; n=2,6,10 controls are included. Arbitrary scalar projectors contribute exactly their rank across the active cut, so this is not merely a coordinate-call argument or a finite-search extrapolation.\n\n## What is new\n\nThis closes the explicitly open **finite-torsion** question in #1911 (`712593f8`): its infinite-dihedral Laurent invariant does not descend through `r^n=1`. The new finite cut invariant does. In particular #1821's D16 central pair retains its 16-call minimum with arbitrarily many dirty roles, and the theorem covers all even periods and heterogeneous endpoints.\n\nThe all-register **D8 central-target** special case was already proved by steph4n-gh in #1573; it is credited, not claimed anew. Arham Tahir's #1919 rank-bounded D8 invocation tariff is distinct and also credited. #1792's stale-handoff warning was checked, along with local/merged #1443 and live all-state overlap.\n\n## Evidence and reproduction\n\nOnly `research/finite-dihedral-cut-bound/` is added: written proof, standard-library verifier, deterministic receipt, full chooser selection, TASK/SOURCES/VERIFY metadata, and actual validation record.\n\n```sh\nuv run --python 3.12 --no-project python -I -B research/finite-dihedral-cut-bound/verify.py\nuv run --python 3.12 --no-project python scripts/research_package.py --package research/finite-dihedral-cut-bound\n```\n\nBoth direct and registered optimized replay passed on macOS arm64 / **CPython 3.12.12**:\n\n- 1,027 complete formal forward/inverse dirty fixtures; 134,864 faithful-regular basis columns.\n- 9,420 complete short mixed words, 47,872 representation products, and 1,088 target cut ranks.\n- 3,720 rank-one call checks over all 155 projectors through four roles; all 174 scalar invertible maps through three roles; 1,088 additional arbitrary-idempotent rank checks.\n- 9,249 omitted-call controls and 13 semantic/model controls reject; a corrupted receipt also rejects under `-O`.\n- A nonzero invertible dirty shear hidden by **both** proof representations is detected by full formal and faithful-regular checks, guarding against represented-endpoint false positives.\n\nThe universal theorem is the written proof, not inferred from finite checks. No native supplier, selected cold replay, hosted CI, Lean, or independent human proof review is claimed.\n\n## Provenance and limits\n\n- Exact base: `0b1cccf86e2f5132ed286a26ef0a681a495740ea`.\n- Independently sampled seed: `63537661f5ee8acaa486b70f82726911`; track `bit-mechanisms`; emitted card `bit-new-register-model`; target-specific relaxation method.\n- [Pre-computation claim](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1929#discussioncomment-18925900).\n- Final shared overlap snapshot covered 584 open PRs and 565 discussion records, fully paginated; exact live predecessor heads and all-state dihedral search checked. This is not a novelty guarantee.\n\n**No new kappa or selected-supplier change.** Odd periods, arbitrary free address maps, nonlinear invocation tariffs, and physical/compiler/frame/hosting/precision/tape admission remain outside scope. The lower bound grants scalar mixing free only as an optimistic formal relaxation; the attaining word needs no clean state, extra workspace, COPY, or uncharged restoration.\n\nPrepared for Adam Gall with substantial OpenAI Codex GPT-6 Astra assistance in proof, implementation and writing. Apache-2.0; predecessor credit retained.\n",
